Olist E-Commerce Data Engineering & Analytics Platform
An end-to-end Snowflake data engineering project that transforms raw Brazilian e-commerce data into a structured analytical data warehouse with layered architecture, dimensional modeling, and business-ready analytics views.

Snowflake SQL Data Engineering Star Schema

Project Overview
This project implements a complete data engineering pipeline on Snowflake using the Brazilian Olist E-Commerce dataset. It demonstrates how fragmented transactional data spread across 9 CSV files can be ingested, cleaned, modeled, and transformed into a structured analytical system.

Raw Olist CSV Files (9 datasets)
        ↓
  Snowflake Load (via Snowsight)
        ↓
  RAW Layer (as-is source data)
        ↓
  STAGING Layer (cleaned + standardized)
        ↓
  CORE Layer (Star Schema - Facts + Dimensions)
        ↓
  ANALYTICS Layer (Business-ready views)
        ↓
  Power BI / Reporting
The pipeline processes 5,732 records across 9 source tables into a dimensional model with 1 fact table, 4 dimension tables, and 6 analytical views.

Project Objectives
Build a multi-layer Snowflake data warehouse (RAW → STAGING → CORE → ANALYTICS)
Ingest and validate 9 related e-commerce datasets
Clean and standardize raw data with computed business metrics
Design and implement a Star Schema for analytical workloads
Create business-ready views for revenue, delivery, seller, and customer analysis
Apply Snowflake best practices: warehouse sizing, auto-suspend, IF NOT EXISTS safety
Leverage Time Travel for data recovery and protection
Business Problem
Raw e-commerce data is distributed across multiple disconnected datasets — customers, orders, payments, products, sellers, and reviews live in separate files with no unified view. This makes answering basic business questions difficult:

What is the total revenue and how does it trend monthly?
Which product categories generate the most revenue?
Which sellers perform best by revenue and customer satisfaction?
How long do deliveries take, and how often are they late?
What payment methods do customers prefer?
How does review score correlate with delivery performance?
This project transforms fragmented source data into a single analytical system where all these questions can be answered with simple SQL queries against pre-built views.

