Interview Talking Points
30-Second Pitch
"I built an end-to-end data engineering project on Snowflake using the Brazilian Olist e-commerce dataset. I designed a 4-layer architecture — RAW, STAGING, CORE, and ANALYTICS — transforming 9 source CSV files into a Star Schema with pre-built analytical views. The pipeline is idempotent, cost-optimized, and produces business-ready insights on revenue, delivery performance, and customer satisfaction."

1-Minute Technical Explanation
"The project starts with 9 raw CSV files from the Olist marketplace. I loaded them into Snowflake's RAW schema as-is. The STAGING layer applies cleaning — standardizing text, computing delivery days, joining category translations. The CORE layer implements a Star Schema with FACT_ORDER_ITEMS at order-item grain, joined to four dimensions: customers, products, sellers, and a generated date dimension. Finally, the ANALYTICS layer has six pre-aggregated views covering monthly revenue, category performance, seller scorecards, customer lifetime value, delivery SLA tracking, and payment analysis. I used Time Travel to recover from an accidental data wipe, and redesigned the pipeline with IF NOT EXISTS to be safely re-runnable."

Technical Questions You Can Answer
Question	Key Points
"Why did you choose a Star Schema?"	Optimized for analytical queries, reduces join complexity, maps to business thinking, works well with BI tools
"How do you handle pipeline failures?"	IF NOT EXISTS protects RAW data; STAGING/CORE use OR REPLACE for idempotent reruns; Time Travel enables recovery
"What's the grain of your fact table?"	One row per order item — allows analysis at product, seller, and order level
"How do you handle data quality?"	NULL filtering, TRIM/INITCAP for consistency, COALESCE for missing translations, referential joins with LEFT JOIN
"Why four separate schemas?"	Separation of concerns — RAW preserves source truth, STAGING handles cleaning, CORE models business logic, ANALYTICS serves consumers
